"answer_en": "At sea level the blood delivers to the tissues about 38 percent of the maximum oxygen it could carry. On abrupt arrival at 4,500 metres the lower pO2 reduces delivery to about 30 percent. Within a few hours the concentration of 2,3-bisphosphoglycerate in the erythrocytes begins to rise, from about 5 millimolar to about 8 millimolar. BPG binds one molecule per haemoglobin tetramer, in the positively charged cavity between the two beta subunits, a pocket that exists only in the T state and disappears on the transition to R. By stabilising T it lowers haemoglobin's affinity for oxygen. Because the lung end of the curve is close to saturation anyway, this has only a small effect on loading but a considerable effect on unloading, and delivery is restored to about 37 percent — nearly the sea-level value. The change reverses on return to sea level, and the same rise in BPG occurs in hypoxia caused by inadequate lung or circulatory function.",
"answer_cn": "在海平面，血液向组织输送的氧约为其所能携带最大量的38%。突然抵达4,500米后，较低的pO2使输送量降至约30%。几小时之内，红细胞内2,3-二磷酸甘油酸的浓度开始上升，从约5毫摩尔升至约8毫摩尔。BPG按每个血红蛋白四聚体结合一分子的比例，结合在两个β亚基之间那个带正电荷的空腔中；该口袋只在T态下存在，一旦转变为R态便消失。通过稳定T态，它降低了血红蛋白对氧的亲和力。由于曲线的肺端本来就接近饱和，这对装载只有很小的影响，对卸载的影响却相当可观，于是输送量恢复到约37%——几乎是海平面的水平。返回海平面后这一变化会逆转；而在肺或循环功能不足所致的缺氧中，BPG也会发生同样的升高。"
