"why_en": "Influenza attaches through the HA lectin, but budding virions leave wrapped in host plasma membrane and would remain stuck to the cell and to each other; a viral sialidase (neuraminidase) trims the terminal sialic acid, releasing the particles and preventing aggregation, so another round of infection can begin. Oseltamivir and zanamivir are sugar analogues that inhibit the viral sialidase by competing with the host cell oligosaccharides for binding, which both prevents release and causes viral particles to aggregate. On resistance, Figure 7-30 is explicit: oseltamivir fits the site by pushing a nearby Glu out of the way, and a mutation replacing a His near this Glu with the larger side chain of a Tyr means the drug binds much less well, making the mutant virus effectively resistant. Option 2 names the wrong viral protein and puts the mutation in the host. Option 3 describes an entirely different drug class. Option 4 invents a cross-linking mechanism.",
