"why_en": "**Box** 10-1 gives both diseases side by side, which is exactly why they are easy to swap. Niemann-Pick disease is caused by a rare genetic defect in sphingomyelinase, the enzyme that cleaves phosphocholine from sphingomyelin, so sphingomyelin accumulates in brain, spleen and liver, becomes evident in infants and causes intellectual disability and early death. “ More common is Tay-Sachs disease, in which ganglioside GM2 accumulates in the brain and spleen ” — the sentence carries a figure call-out at that point — “ owing to lack of the enzyme hexosaminidase A ” , with progressive developmental delay and disability, paralysis, blindness, and death by the age of 3 or 4 years (A p.353). The general rule behind both is on the previous page: degradation proceeds bond by bond, with a specific lysosomal hydrolase for each, so a single missing enzyme stalls the pathway and its own substrate accumulates.",
