"why_en": "The definition is given in one sentence at the head of the steroid-hormone subsection: “ Steroids are oxidized derivatives of sterols; they have the sterol nucleus but lack the alkyl chain attached to ring D of cholesterol, and they are more polar than cholesterol ” (A p.356). Three claims: oxidised, nucleus retained, side chain lost — and the third is the discriminating one, since the first is a matter of degree and the second is shared. The greater polarity then follows from the first and third together, more oxygen and less hydrocarbon. Czech 8-2-4-4 gives the complementary definition from the sterol side: a hydrocarbon side chain of 8 to 10 carbons at position 17 plus a hydroxyl at position 3 is what marks a sterol. Test both on **figure** 10-18: testosterone, beta-estradiol, cortisol and aldosterone all lack the long C-17 tail and all carry oxygen functions on the rings.",
"why_cn": "**定义写在类固醇激素那一小节开头的一句话里**：**「类固醇是固醇的氧化衍生物；它们具有固醇的核，但缺少连在胆固醇 D 环上的烷基链，而且比胆固醇更极性。」**（A 第 356 页）。**三个主张**：**被氧化、核保留、侧链丢失**——**而第三条才是判别性的**，因为**第一条是程度问题，第二条是共有的**。**「更极性」随后由第一条和第三条合起来推出**：**氧更多，烃更少**。**捷克 `8-2-4-4` 从固醇那一侧给出互补的定义**：**17 位上 8 到 10 个碳的烃侧链，加上 3 位上一个羟基，才是固醇的标志**。**拿 **figure** 10-18 检验两者**：**testosterone、β-estradiol、cortisol、aldosterone 全都没有那条长长的 C-17 尾巴，而且环上都带着含氧官能团。**"
