"why_en": "“Given that most urea cycle steps are irreversible, the absent enzyme activity can often be identified by determining which cycle intermediate is present in elevated concentration in the blood and/or urine” (A p.638). Irreversibility is what makes the accumulation specific: material piles up at the block and cannot run backwards to spread the signal around the ring. Only one step, the argininosuccinase reaction, is reversible. The clinical frame is worth carrying with it: such infants often appear normal at birth and then develop hyperammonemia with cerebral edema, lethargy and hyperventilation, and a protein-free diet is **not** an option, because humans cannot synthesise half of the twenty common amino acids.",
