"answer_en": "First, the rotor's size. One full revolution of the c ring makes three ATP and requires as many protons as there are c subunits, and that number is species-dependent — 8 in cattle, 10 in yeast — so the protons per ATP, and hence the yield, differ between organisms with no reason to land on an integer. Second, the transport tax. Of the four protons that flow in per ATP, one is spent not on synthesis but on carrying phosphate in through the phosphate translocase, while the adenine nucleotide translocase exchanges ADP for ATP at the cost of a net negative charge leaving. A third reason, for cytosolic NADH specifically, is that the shuttle used decides whether the electrons enter at Complex I or at ubiquinone.",
