"why_en": "This is the section's own organising contrast, stated in its opening paragraph and repeated when the pyrimidine pathway begins. For purines the ring structure is built up one or a few atoms at a time while attached to ribose throughout the process, and PRPP is acted on at the very first committed step, when glutamine donates an amino group to its C-1. For pyrimidines the six-membered ring is made first and completed as orotate, and only then is ribose 5-phosphate added to the finished ring by orotate phosphoribosyltransferase, three steps from UMP. The book explicitly warns that the intuitive picture — bases synthesised and then attached to ribose, as might be expected — is wrong for purines. The consequence is worth carrying with the fact: because the purine ring is always attached to sugar, a free purine base is never an intermediate of the de novo route. Free bases occur in nucleotide metabolism only at the degradation end and in the salvage pathways fed by it.",
"why_cn": "这是本节自己的组织性对比，在开篇第一段就说了，等嘧啶路径开始时又重复了一次。**嘌呤**：环结构是**一次几个原子**地搭建起来的，而整个过程中糖一直连在上面；**PRPP 在第一个定向步骤就被动手**——谷氨酰胺把一个氨基送到它的 C-1 上。**嘧啶**：六元环**先造好**、完成为**乳清酸 (orotate)**，**之后**才由**乳清酸磷酸核糖转移酶**把核糖 5-磷酸加到这个造好的环上，此时离 UMP 只剩三步。书里明确警告说，那个直觉图景——「碱基先合成好、再挂到核糖上，尽管你可能会这么以为」——对嘌呤来说是**错的**。有一个后果值得和这个事实一起记住：**正因为嘌呤环始终连着糖，游离嘌呤碱基从来不是从头途径的中间产物**。在核苷酸代谢中，游离碱基只出现在**降解那一端，以及由它喂饭的补救途径**里。"
